Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the walked intersection of a blend with the face at its end ends
where the walk first passes the end point, not where it last comes back
near it (mitcad#343).

ChFi3d_ComputeCurves intersects the blend with the face at the end by a
walk from the middle of the cap (IntWalk_PWalking) where the analytic and
GeomInt intersections fail, and cuts the walked line between its points
nearest to the cap's ends. At an OnDiff end whose contact meets the face
at the end on an edge across which its own face goes on tangentially to
that face (a rounding along an earlier rounding that is tangent to the
face at the end), the blend touches the face at the end at that point: the
two branches of the intersection cross there. The walk passed through the
point onto the other branch, wandered past the end and came back to it;
the cut then kept the wandering part (the cap 4.3e-3 off the surfaces, the
face's wire self-intersecting) where the walk, in the stripe's other
direction, had stopped at the point. The same blend walked the other way
round gave a valid result: a rounding of a band's edge along a cone and an
earlier rounding, whose import named its edges in the other order, was
invalid.

Now the line is cut where it first passes each end: walking from the
start's nearest point, the first point whose distance to the end is a
local minimum no larger than the line's step there ends it (and likewise
for the start, from the end's side). Where the line comes nearest to an
end once, that is the point taken before.

The line also passes an end inside a step whose two points lie on either
side of it: the end's foot on the step falls between them, no further from
the end than the step is long, and the nearer of the two points ends the
line (mitcad#2151). Near a point where the surfaces touch the walk may
step back and forth across it: at the corner of two blends both tangent to
the end face, where their tangent lines on that face meet, the walk stepped
from 0.049 before the point to 0.0037 past it, back to 0.0015 before it,
then 0.025 past it onto the other branch and back near it. The point rule
took the point stepped back to (the first local minimum), so the line kept
the step past the point and back, and the cut edge bulged past the blend's
tangent line (a face unorientable in the shell). The steps hang on the walk's rounding noise: one slab of 202 on
Windows (MSVC), none on Linux.

Upstream: new.

Test: geometry.port.0901
(geometry/tests/port/p0901-cap-line-cut-where-it-first-passes-the-end.cpp):
the roof slab of test_fillet_eave.cpp, 20 to 30 long every 0.1, the tip's
edge, the eave's end edge and the slope's top edge on the end face rounded
with 0.9 and with 1.0 (the three blends cut each other at the tip, their
caps walked): every result one valid solid, on Linux and on Windows.
Without the patch (reverted at the port's tip) 9 and 5 of the 101 slabs
came out invalid on Linux, which of them hanging on the walk's rounding
noise; with the point rule alone, the slab 28.0 long rounded with 1.0 on
Windows.

